[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Jenkins Pipeline: Stages, Steps, When Conditions, and Post Actions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins Declarative Pipelines provide a rich, flexible framework for defining continuous integration and continuous delivery workflows. To build robust and maintainable pipelines, you need to understand the core structural blocks: stages, steps, when conditions, and post actions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Stages and Steps"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A 'stage' block defines a conceptually distinct phase through which the pipeline passes (e.g., Build, Test, Deploy). Inside each stage, you define 'steps', which are the actual atomic commands or tasks executed by Jenkins."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building the application...'
                sh 'mvn clean package'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. When Conditions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'when' directive allows the pipeline to determine whether a stage should be executed based on the given condition. If the condition evaluates to true, the stage runs; otherwise, it is skipped."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"branch: Executes the stage only when building a specific branch (e.g., when { branch 'main' })."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"environment: Runs when a specific environment variable is set to a certain value."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"expression: Evaluates a Groovy script to determine execution."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"stage('Deploy to Production') {
    when {
        branch 'main'
        environment name: 'DEPLOY_ENV', value: 'prod'
    }
    steps {
        echo 'Deploying to production server...'
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Post Actions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'post' section defines actions that run at the end of the pipeline or at the end of a specific stage. It allows you to handle cleanups, notifications, or status-based reporting. Common post conditions include always, success, failure, and unstable."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn test'
            }
        }
    }
    post {
        success {
            echo 'Pipeline completed successfully!'
        }
        failure {
            echo 'Pipeline failed. Sending alert notification.'
        }
        always {
            echo 'Cleaning up workspace workspaces...'
            deleteDir()
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining stages, steps, when conditions, and post actions, you can orchestrate complex enterprise-grade deployment pipelines with fine-grained control over execution flow and error handling."}]
